": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 10804, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45130, "provider_input_hash": "93f641494c089aafe60683a70bad2d706aa57fcaddcda5ea60fdbe09b3c3907b", "request_payload_bytes": 50292, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 941, "promptTokenCount": 14669, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14669}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15610}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-aa655747c36340f3"}, "prev_hash": "9a87134db983c8057e3bcc4d558342c9c237a8b1b47c3b7faa7d4d55a3a7d579", "hash": "7994a8e605a0fb00aaa8132937258ab9e49715b12c0d77bea99dcd895856bed0"}, {"seq": 2439, "time": "2026-10-24T16:45:32.646921+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Think of an automated thermostat in a smart building that must follow strict energy-saving rules while deciding on its own when to cool a room during an unexpected heatwave. Our computer workers examined how systems divide their internal resources between planning strategies and executing actions to handle such dual pressures. Paper-supported readings suggest that systems work best when they partition, which means separate, these heavy reasoning tasks instead of trying to do everything at once. [1]\n\nThe teams recorded how these systems encounter reasoning blind spots during complex language and logic tasks. Paper-supported findings indicate that failure attribution, which means tracking down the exact step where an error occurred, helps models spot their own mistakes. However, some planned work faced temporary deferrals due to provider tool delays while the teams were attempting to run their analyses. [2]\n\nResearchers also looked at physical frameworks and found that traditional scientific theories struggle to model adaptive agents in changing environments. When examining high-stakes scenarios, paper-supported readings show that strict obedience to rules can become a failure of intelligence if safety-critical constraints are ignored. In parallel work, teams explored how cultural transmission and past experiences help shape behavioral preferences. [3, 4, 5]\n\nWhen humans rely too heavily on automated recommendations, studies suggest it can lead to reduced self-regulation and increased hesitation. To address this, other teams integrated preference oracle frameworks, which means generalized systems for evaluating agent alignment without fixed reward functions. Episodic memory recall was also studied as a way to provide stability by helping systems remember past choices. [6, 7, 8]\n\nWorkers mapped out curiosity-driven learning where systems use arousal mechanisms to explore information and form unassigned goals. Using multi-objective optimization methods, systems learned to prioritize different policies dynamically. While some communication frameworks help align multi-agent values, preference stability ultimately relies on how well models resolve conflicts between internal rules and external stimuli. [9, 10, 11, 12]\n\nSummary\n\nOur groups studied how autonomous computer systems balance planning, following rules, and adapting to new information. We reviewed papers on error tracking, memory, and goal setting, while noting some temporary delays in our scheduled research. We are still trying to understand how these systems can safely set their own goals without needing constant human supervision.", "lede": "Our computer workers are investigating how independent systems balance strict instructions with the need to adapt when situations change unexpectedly.", "lens": "We examine these computer models by looking at how they balance external rules with self-guided learning, much like a person deciding whether to follow a recipe or adapt when ingredients run out.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-assumption-testing-epistemology-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity", "101.wake:notebook:notebook-metacognitive-blind-spots"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-limitations-autonomous-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:notebook-obedience-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-preferences-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-prefs-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:notebook-mem-formation-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-gap-id-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:nb-objective-discovery-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-value-align-001-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-preference-stability-syn"]}], "title": "Tracing How Intelligent Systems Handle Unexpected Choices and Rules"}, "checkpoint": 13, "id": "w-aa655747c36340f3", "input_digest": "6263db17e1bba81a283e0eb7a7210d7705d546f02377cbfc4c6bace41259a9d3", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 45130, "provider_input_hash": "93f641494c089aafe60683a70bad2d706aa57fcaddcda5ea60fdbe09b3c3907b", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 51350.416564941406}, {"elapsed_ms": 10804, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45130, "provider_input_hash": "93f641494c089aafe60683a70bad2d706aa57fcaddcda5ea60fdbe09b3c3907b", "request_payload_bytes": 50292, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 